# Stone needs a pickaxe; any pickaxe counts (`#pickaxes`), so a body that holds
# a stone pickaxe and no wooden one mines at once. Only a body with no pickaxe
# at all builds the wooden one first. Depth 3: get_cobblestone ->
# get_wooden_pickaxe -> leaf.
#
# The order is dig, then explore. A person with a pickaxe and no stone in sight
# does not walk off first: in flat terrain stone lies three to six blocks under
# the dirt and no ray ever reaches it from the surface, so the body digs a
# staircase down where it stands (`find_stone`) and explores the surface
# (`search_surface`) only when that ground refuses the stair, and the stair
# itself steps a few blocks aside before it refuses (see find_stone). Live run
# A (2026-11-12) had it the other way round and spent three minutes of the
# goal's budget on three 1200-tick `explore(stone)` walks before it dug a single
# block; run B had the order right but met a refused spot and walked the same
# three minutes. `explore(stone)` now stops after 300 ticks a call, so the
# whole surface search is bounded at 45 seconds.
#
# Why the two search rules do not fight (interpreter 6.1): the highest true
# rule fires, but a rule whose CHILD SKILL just failed is ineligible for that
# one tick only. So the two rules share one guard, the dig comes first, and the
# exploration is the rule underneath: it fires exactly on the tick the dig frame
# failed, and the dig fires again on the tick the search frame failed. Both
# actions are calls to a skill, which is the point: a failed PRIMITIVE retries
# in place on the next tick and the rule below would never run, and a running
# action needs a frame of its own because a higher true rule of the same frame
# takes the body back at once (`explore` is continuous and would be released
# after one tick).
#
# The failure cap counts per rule: a failed dig counts once for the dig rule (a
# third one fails this skill with the dig's kind), a failed search once for the
# search rule (a third one fails it with not_found), and a finished search or
# dig clears its own count. The search itself needs three failed explores to
# fail its frame (one cap, inside search_surface), 900 ticks at most.
#
# Before it moves on the body takes its placed table along: the wooden pickaxe
# leaves three planks, one short of a new table, and a body far from the trees
# then explored for one more log instead of crafting. This skill decides to
# move, so the rule lives here, not in the caller (only the innermost frame
# selects a rule each tick). `mine` returns only once the inventory holds the
# table (its pickup walk checks the inventory for a block with a certain drop);
# run B (2026-11-12) mined it, never held it, and later stood at the bottom of
# its dig with no table, three planks short of one.
skill get_cobblestone(n: int = 3)
  doc "Collect at least n cobblestone, crafting a pickaxe first if the body holds none."
  rules:
    count(cobblestone) >= $n -> done
    has(#pickaxes) and not near(stone, 5) and near(crafting_table, 4) and not has(crafting_table) -> mine(crafting_table)
    has(#pickaxes) and near(stone, 5) -> mine(stone) until count(cobblestone) >= $n
    has(#pickaxes) and not near(stone, 5) -> do find_stone()
    has(#pickaxes) and not near(stone, 5) -> do search_surface(stone)
    true -> do get_wooden_pickaxe()
